  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}=1.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-4),\\,a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2},\\,b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-4) & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}})(x-0) \\\\ y+4 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
